                        TEMPEST MIDI v1.112026
                        developped by beemo <3

  README.TXT

  Convert MIDI files into POLYPHONIC TEMPEST VIDEOS. When one of these
  videos plays fullscreen over HDMI, the cable's unintended electromagnetic
  emissions carry the music, and any AM-capable receiver can pick it up
  (SDR with gqrx / SDR++, HackRF PortaPack, ...).

  This project is higly inspired by erikyyy 's Tempest for Eliza
  http://www.erikyyy.de/tempest/ : every scanline is full-width white or
  black, for maximum emitted power. On top of that it adds POLYPHONY and
  pitch derived from the real video line rate.

  HOW IT WORKS

  White and black scanlines encode to different HDMI TMDS symbol streams, so
  they radiate differently at harmonics of the pixel clock. Alternating white
  and black bands of rows therefore switches the emission on and off at an
  audio rate. That's AM, and the band period sets the pitch:

  one scanline = one audio sample   1920x1080@60: 1125 * 60 = 67500 lines/s

  row 0   |################################|  on    every row is full width
  row 1   |################################|  on    (max power, like Eliza)
  row 2   |                                |  off
  row 3   |                                |  off
  ...      period (in rows) = line_rate / pitch

  Voices are mixed per row, always full width (--mix):

  sd (default)
    What each row does   sum of the voices' squares, normalized to the
                         active voices, converted to 1 bit by sigma-delta
    Power per voice      1 voice = full Eliza square; a chord shares the
                         full swing
    Notes                sigma-delta noise sits near 30 kHz, above the
                         receiver's audio band

  arp
    What each row does   one voice per frame, rotating (chiptune arpeggio
                         at 60 Hz)
    Power per voice      full for every note
    Notes                chords sound like arpeggios

  rgb
    What each row does   voice i on color i % 3: red, green, blue rows
    Power per voice      each voice owns a whole TMDS lane
    Notes                experimental: lanes may add or partly cancel at the
                         receiver; uses yuv444p

  - Phase is computed from the absolute line time, vertical blanking included,
    so there's no per-frame reset and no 60 Hz buzz when the player is in sync.
  - Percussion (GM channel 10) is dropped. When more notes are held than there
    are voices, the highest pitches win.
  - Velocity is ignored by default, so every note is at full power. Use
    --velocity to honor it.

  INSTALL

    python3 -m venv .venv
    .venv/bin/pip install -e '.[dev]'

  ffmpeg is bundled through imageio-ffmpeg, so no system install is needed.

  USAGE

    tempestmidi search mario         # search bitmidi.com
    tempestmidi fetch 72257          # download into ~/.cache/tempestmidi/
    tempestmidi convert bitmidi:72257 -o mario.mp4
    tempestmidi convert song.mid -o song.mp4 --voices 3 --preview-audio
    tempestmidi convert song.mid -o song_arp.mp4 --mix arp
    tempestmidi convert song.mid -o song_rgb.mp4 --mix rgb --voices 3

    # Example on 59.94 Hz display
    tempestmidi convert song.mid -o song.mp4 --mode 1920x1080x1125@60000/1001

  --voices N                   (default: 4)
      max simultaneous voices (fewer voices = more power each in sd)

  --mix sd|arp|rgb             (default: sd)
      voice mixing, see above

  --velocity                   (default: off)
      scale level by note velocity (less power)

  --mode WxHxVTOTAL@REFRESH    (default: 1920x1080x1125@60)
      must match the display mode used for playback, since pitch depends on
      the line rate

  --preview-audio              (default: off)
      adds an audio track with the expected demodulated mix (for humans;
      mute it during reception if you like)

  Output is H.264 at 60 fps CFR with the deblocking filter disabled, so band
  edges stay sharp: yuv420p (High) for sd/arp, and lossless yuv444p (High
  4:4:4 Predictive, crf 0) for rgb, so adjacent rows don't share chroma.

  PLAYBACK REQUIREMENTS

  - FULLSCREEN, AT NATIVE RESOLUTION, WITH NO SCALING. Use the same resolution
    as --mode.
  - THE DISPLAY REFRESH RATE MUST MATCH THE VIDEO: 60 Hz for the default mode.
    If your output runs at 59.94 Hz, convert with
    --mode 1920x1080x1125@60000/1001.
  - Use a player that doesn't drop or repeat frames, e.g.
    mpv --fs --video-sync=display-resample or mpv --fs. Every dropped frame
    causes a short glitch.
  - Use full-range RGB output and turn off any GPU dithering or "enhancement"
    features. They change the pixel values that get TMDS-encoded.
  - Volume and quality are best with nothing else on screen: no OSD, no mouse
    cursor.

  RECEIVING

  - The signal is AM, so use AM mode with about 10 kHz bandwidth.
  - Tune near a harmonic of the pixel clock. For 1080p60 that's
    148.5 MHz x k (148.5, 297, 445.5, 594, ... MHz). The strongest harmonic
    depends on your cable and GPU, so scan around them on a waterfall.
  - Start with the antenna close to the HDMI cable (cm to 1 m).
  - If examples/tempest_elize_song.mp4 is audible on your setup, the same
    frequency works for tempestmidi videos.

  Tested setups

  RECEIVER                            FREQUENCY      NOTES
  pending hardware test (task 6.1)

  SHOWCASE

  Iconic and geek tunes that work well, rendered in the two modes that
  sounded best on hardware (arp with 6 voices, rgb with 3):

  TUNE                                      BITMIDI  LENGTH  NOTES DROPPED
                                            ID               ARP6 / RGB3
  Legend of Zelda - Overworld               68140    72 s    0 / 0
  Tetris - Main Theme                       100444   34 s    0 / 0
  Sonic - Green Hill Zone                   95742    90 s    0 / 80
  Pirates of the Caribbean - He's a Pirate  85261    78 s    6 / 425
  Star Wars - Imperial March                29414    106 s   22 / 232
  Final Fantasy XIII - Prelude              46512    77 s    0 / 31
  Portal - Still Alive                      86046    175 s   0 / 18
  Nyan Cat                                  59815    113 s   0 / 0
  Skyrim - Sons of Skyrim                   101127   101 s   0 / 344

  To regenerate them (videos are git-ignored):

    for s in "zelda_overworld 68140" "tetris 100444" \
             "sonic_greenhill 95742" "pirates 85261" \
             "imperial_march 29414" "ff13_prelude 46512" \
             "portal_still_alive 86046" "nyan_cat 59815" \
             "skyrim_sons 101127"; do
      set -- $s
      tempestmidi convert bitmidi:$2 -o showcase/$1_arp6.mp4 \
          --mix arp --voices 6 --preview-audio
      tempestmidi convert bitmidi:$2 -o showcase/$1_rgb3.mp4 \
          --mix rgb --voices 3 --preview-audio
    done

  The voice cap keeps the highest notes, so dense arrangements lose their
  lower parts in rgb with 3 voices. Use --voices 6 there for fuller harmony:
  lanes then carry 2 voices each.

  REPOSITORY LAYOUT

    tempestmidi/   source.py (bitmidi + MIDI), voices.py, render.py,
                   export.py, cli.py
    tests/         pytest suite
    examples/      reference Tempest-for-Eliza HDMI video
                   (see examples/README.md)
          
  DEVELOPMENT

    .venv/bin/pytest -q

  The tests cover bitmidi (mocked HTTP), MIDI normalization, voice
  allocation, renderer mix modes, pitch and phase accuracy, and mp4 round
  trips: decode the file, compare rows and channels, and measure the band
  pitch.


